Control plane and reconciliation|Kubernetes is a declarative orchestration system whose controllers reconcile observed state toward desired state stored through the API.|The API server validates requests, etcd persists cluster state, controllers manage resource lifecycles, and the scheduler assigns unscheduled Pods. Reconciliation is asynchronous; creating an object does not guarantee its workload is already ready.|kubectl get --raw='/readyz?verbose'
Pods and containers|A Pod is Kubernetes' scheduling unit containing one or more cooperating containers.|Containers in a Pod share its network namespace and can share declared volumes. Pods are disposable and normally replaced by controllers rather than repaired in place. A Pod IP is not a stable service address.|kubectl get pods -n NAMESPACE -o wide
Deployments and ReplicaSets|A Deployment manages ReplicaSets to maintain and update replicated stateless Pods.|Changing the Pod template creates a new rollout. Rolling-update settings control surge and unavailable capacity. A ReplicaSet count does not prove application readiness; inspect conditions, readiness and available replicas.|kubectl rollout status deployment/APP -n NAMESPACE --timeout=60s
StatefulSets|A StatefulSet manages Pods with stable identities and ordered lifecycle behavior appropriate to some stateful applications.|Each ordinal can retain a distinct volume claim and network identity. The application still owns replication and data consistency. Replacing a Pod does not automatically repair corrupted data or guarantee a safe database failover.|kubectl get statefulsets -n NAMESPACE
DaemonSets|A DaemonSet maintains a Pod on eligible nodes, commonly for networking, logs or host agents.|Node selectors, affinity and tolerations influence eligibility. A daemon rollout can affect every node, so monitor availability and resource use. DaemonSets are not a substitute for ordinary replicated application Deployments.|kubectl get daemonsets -n NAMESPACE
Jobs and CronJobs|A Job manages work intended to complete; a CronJob creates Jobs according to a schedule.|Retries, deadlines and concurrency policy affect execution. Work should tolerate duplicate or retried execution where applicable. Successful Pod completion is not proof that an external business operation occurred exactly once.|kubectl get jobs,cronjobs -n NAMESPACE
Namespaces|Namespaces scope names and selected policies for groups of Kubernetes objects.|They help organize workloads and apply quotas or permissions, but are not complete security boundaries by themselves. Some resources are cluster-scoped. Network and identity isolation require additional controls.|kubectl get namespaces
Labels and selectors|Labels attach identifying attributes to objects; selectors choose objects by those attributes.|Services and controllers rely on matching selectors, so accidental label changes can disconnect traffic or change ownership behavior. Use stable labels for identity and separate changing metadata. A matching label does not imply a Pod is ready.|kubectl get pods -n NAMESPACE --show-labels
Services and EndpointSlices|A Service provides a stable access abstraction for selected backends; EndpointSlices describe backend addresses and readiness information.|Selectors commonly populate EndpointSlices from Pods. Service port and targetPort serve different roles. When traffic fails, verify selectors, ready endpoints and the application's actual listening port before changing exposure type.|kubectl get endpointslices -n NAMESPACE
Cluster DNS|Cluster DNS provides service discovery names within the Kubernetes networking environment.|Pod resolver configuration and search domains influence name lookup. Service discovery requires DNS health as well as correctly named resources. A successful lookup establishes an address, not application reachability or readiness.|kubectl get service kube-dns -n kube-system
Ingress and Gateway|Ingress and Gateway API resources describe routing from clients toward services through implementing controllers.|Creating a routing object alone does not install a controller or load balancer. Listener, hostname, route attachment, TLS and backend health all affect delivery. Supported features depend on the chosen implementation.|kubectl get ingress -n NAMESPACE
NetworkPolicy|NetworkPolicy declares allowed traffic relationships for Pods when enforced by a supporting network plugin.|Ingress and egress isolation are evaluated separately, and allowed policies combine. Include required DNS and dependency paths when restricting egress. A policy object without an enforcing implementation does not block packets.|kubectl get networkpolicy -n NAMESPACE
ConfigMaps|A ConfigMap stores non-secret configuration data separately from container images.|Values can be injected as environment variables or mounted files. Environment variables do not update in an already running container when the ConfigMap changes. Application reload behavior and mount update semantics determine when changes take effect.|kubectl get configmaps -n NAMESPACE
Secrets|A Secret stores sensitive configuration values for authorized workload access.|Base64 representation is encoding, not encryption. Control RBAC, encryption-at-rest configuration, mount exposure and log handling. Updating a Secret does not guarantee every application immediately reloads its credentials.|kubectl get secrets -n NAMESPACE
Service accounts|A ServiceAccount identifies a workload for Kubernetes API authentication and related identity integration.|RBAC determines what the identity can do. Avoid mounting credentials where API access is unnecessary, and use appropriately scoped, bounded tokens. A namespace's default service account should not silently become a broad administrative identity.|kubectl get serviceaccounts -n NAMESPACE
RBAC|Role-based access control authorizes API operations through roles and bindings.|Roles define verbs on resources, while bindings grant those permissions to subjects. Namespace and cluster scope matter. Check the exact verb, resource and identity rather than treating authentication success as authorization.|kubectl auth can-i get pods -n NAMESPACE
Requests and limits|Resource requests inform scheduling and resource sharing; limits constrain resource use according to the resource type.|CPU limits may throttle runnable work, while memory pressure against a limit can lead to OOM termination. Current usage is not the scheduler's capacity calculation. Compare workload demand, requests, limits and node conditions.|kubectl top pods -n NAMESPACE
Scheduling and affinity|The scheduler selects a feasible node for an unscheduled Pod using resource and placement constraints.|Requests, affinity, topology rules, volumes and other filters affect feasibility. Hard constraints can leave Pods Pending even when aggregate capacity looks sufficient. Inspect scheduling events before relaxing intended placement rules.|kubectl describe pod POD -n NAMESPACE
Taints and tolerations|Taints repel Pods from nodes unless matching tolerations permit the placement.|A toleration permits scheduling but does not attract a Pod to that node. Combine with affinity when dedicated placement is required. Effects such as NoSchedule and NoExecute influence new placement and eviction differently.|kubectl describe node NODE
Readiness liveness and startup probes|Probes communicate whether a container is ready for traffic, needs restart, or is still starting.|Readiness failures remove a Pod from ordinary ready service backends; liveness failure can restart the container. Startup probes delay other probes until startup succeeds. A dependency outage should not automatically cause a fleet-wide restart loop.|kubectl describe pod POD -n NAMESPACE
Persistent volumes and claims|A PersistentVolume represents storage capacity; a PersistentVolumeClaim requests storage for a workload.|Binding, access modes, topology and reclaim policy affect allocation and lifecycle. A claim can remain Pending because no suitable storage is provisioned. Deleting workload objects and deleting data are separate operations with policy-dependent consequences.|kubectl get pvc -n NAMESPACE
StorageClasses and CSI|A StorageClass describes provisioning parameters, while CSI drivers implement storage operations.|Binding mode can coordinate volume placement with Pod scheduling. Driver health, permissions and zone constraints influence provisioning and attachment. A provisioned volume still needs a valid filesystem and application-consistent data procedures.|kubectl get storageclasses
Horizontal Pod Autoscaling|An HPA adjusts replica counts from configured metrics and scaling rules.|Metrics availability, resource requests, stabilization and replica bounds affect decisions. More replicas only help if the bottleneck can scale horizontally. Inspect HPA conditions and downstream capacity before raising maximum replicas.|kubectl describe hpa APP -n NAMESPACE
Disruption budgets|A PodDisruptionBudget limits selected voluntary disruptions according to desired availability.|It can constrain eviction during maintenance but does not prevent all failures or guarantee application quorum. Too-strict budgets can block drains. Align the selector and availability setting with replica count and application behavior.|kubectl get pdb -n NAMESPACE
Node lifecycle and drain|Node lifecycle operations control scheduling and safely remove workloads during maintenance.|Cordon prevents new ordinary scheduling; drain uses eviction subject to relevant constraints. DaemonSets, local data and disruption budgets require explicit handling. Confirm replacement capacity before removing a node from service.|kubectl get nodes -o wide
Security contexts|Security contexts configure process identity and privilege-related runtime settings for Pods or containers.|Non-root execution, capability reduction, read-only roots and seccomp profiles restrict different behaviors. Volume ownership and application write paths must remain compatible. Avoid privileged mode as a general fix for permission errors.|kubectl get pod POD -n NAMESPACE -o yaml
Admission and policy|Admission evaluates API requests after authentication and authorization and before persistence.|Built-in controllers and webhooks can validate or mutate objects. A failing webhook can block otherwise authorized changes depending on policy. Diagnose rejection messages and webhook availability rather than broadly disabling safeguards.|
Rollouts and rollback|A rollout replaces workload revisions; rollback returns the Pod template to an earlier revision when available.|Deployment success reflects controller progress, not every business transaction. Data migrations and external configuration may not roll back with the template. Compare application health and dependency compatibility before and after recovery.|kubectl rollout history deployment/APP -n NAMESPACE
Observability and events|Kubernetes events, logs and metrics expose different parts of workload behavior.|Events describe controller and node observations but expire; container logs show application output; metrics reveal trends. Correlate them by object, timestamp and revision. Absence of retained events does not prove an incident never occurred.|kubectl get events -n NAMESPACE --sort-by=.lastTimestamp
etcd and disaster recovery|etcd stores Kubernetes cluster state, while application data usually lives in separate storage systems.|Protect and test control-plane backups using the distribution's supported procedures. Restoring etcd does not restore every database volume or external cloud dependency. Recovery plans must cover certificates, identities, networking and application data as well as API objects.|
